messages:
laura: Hey — CI started failing intermittently for our JS SDK tests that run under the edge runtime. Jest error is `SyntaxError: Cannot use import statement outside a module` coming from a tiny helper file we mock. package.json has \"type\": \"module\" and tests are .ts. Repro locally is flaky (passes some runs). Any quick guesses?

aj: Do you have transforms configured? Jest needs help for ESM + TS. Two common options: `ts-jest` or `esbuild-jest`. Also consider `extensionsToTreatAsEsm: ['.ts']` if you ship ESM.

laura: We have a basic jest config, no transforms (we relied on esbuild in other scripts). I tried `ts-jest` but then runtime complains about `jest-environment-node` mismatch when running edge-shim.

mika: Edge runtime shim + JEST = messy. A few pointers that helped our SDK: set `moduleResolution` to `nodenext` in tsconfig, add `extensionsToTreatAsEsm: ['.ts']`, and use `esbuild-jest` (lighter than ts-jest). Also, if you mock ESM imports you must use the ESM mock APIs. Example snippet that worked for us:

```json
{
  "transform": {
    "^.+\\.tsx?$": ["esbuild-jest", {"loader": "tsx"}]
  },
  "extensionsToTreatAsEsm": [".ts"],
  "moduleNameMapper": {
    "^@/lib/(.*)$": "<rootDir>/src/lib/$1"
  }
}
```

aj: Another gotcha: importing the module before you call `jest.unstable_mockModule()` will break mocks for ESM. For ESM tests you might need to `await import()` inside the test after setting up mocks. Example pattern:

```js
// top of test file (node 18+ / jest 29+)
await jest.unstable_mockModule('../runtime/shim', () => ({ default: {} }));
const { default: shim } = await import('../runtime/shim');
```

sam: Also check `transformIgnorePatterns` — some packages published as ESM need to be transformed (e.g., `node_modules/@unfriendly/lib`). We saw flakes when CI picked a different node image that resolves ESM differently. Adding those libs to transform helped.

laura: Oof, that `unstable_mockModule` note is probably it — our tests use `jest.resetModules()` and then do a plain `require()` which will break for ESM. Can we avoid requiring and instead dynamic-import after mocks?

mika: Yup. Either switch to dynamic `import()` + `jest.unstable_mockModule()` or move to Vitest for edge-like runtime (vitest plays nicer with ESM and Vite aliases). But if you stay on Jest, try the esbuild-jest + extensionsToTreatAsEsm route first.

devex-bot: CI: build/edge-tests #342 failed — https://ci.internal/reports/342 (failing on import syntax) :face_with_thermometer:

laura: Thanks — will try the dynamic import + esbuild-jest configs. If it still flakes I'll paste the failing log. Appreciate the quick tips! :pray:
